{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 3, :comments ["<b>And Abram said: Look, to me, etc.</b> It is difficult: (a) How could he say <b>You have not given</b>, etc., when He had already promised him and said to him (Genesis 13:16) ״וְשַׂמְתִּי זַרְעֲךָ כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ״ — and what place does he have to question, heaven forbid, the words of HaShem? (b) His saying ״הֵן לִי״ — he should have said ״הֵן לֹא נָתַתָּ לִי״. (c) One needs to know the intention in his saying <b>and behold, the son of my house inherits me</b>: since he has no seed, what difference does it make to him whether the one who inherits him is the son of his house or someone else?" "<b>Indeed</b>, the intention of the verse is along these lines: because in the tidings of the seed that HaShem had told him, He said ״כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ״, and it is well known that those likened to the dust of the earth are lowly and base people who possess no holy soul, in the manner of what is said (Ecclesiastes 3:21) ״רוּחַ הַבְּהֵמָה הַיּוֹרֶדֶת הִיא לְמַטָּה לָאָרֶץ״, and they are what are called עַמֵּי הָאָרֶץ — and with people such as these, the desire of the righteous would not be fulfilled. Therefore Abraham said <b>הֵן לִי</b>, etc., meaning: something of worth to me — You have not given seed. And to this HaShem answered him and said: Count the stars, so shall your seed be — and these are the righteous, who are likened to stars, as it is written (Daniel 12:3) ״וּמַצְדִּיקֵי הָרַבִּים כַכּוֹכָבִים״, and with this his mind was set at rest. And do not find it difficult to reconcile this explanation with the statement of our Sages of blessed memory, who expounded the verse ״וְהָיָה זַרְעֲךָ כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ״ that HaShem said to Jacob — and this is their language (Genesis Rabbah, parashah 69): ״כַּעֲפַר״ etc. — just as dust is blessed only with water, so too your children, etc.; just as dust consumes metal vessels, etc. — end quote. Behold, they expounded it not in this manner for the praise that is in dust, but only after we find that HaShem likened them to stars — from this you learn that He did not liken them to dust afterward except for the praise that is in dust, not for the aspect of lowliness. And accordingly you will find that they did not expound thus on the first verse spoken to Abraham, but on the verse spoken to Jacob — after Abraham had already been promised כַּכּוֹכָבִים. And this is why Abraham was troubled by the first likeness, כַּעֲפַר הָאָרֶץ. And with this, two of the difficulties are resolved: why Abraham was not satisfied with the first promise, and also the difficulty of his saying <b>הֵן לִי</b>." "And his saying <b>and behold, the son of my house inherits me</b> may be explained in two ways: <b>The first</b> — that if He would give him only כְּעָפָר (like dust), he would prefer the son of his house to inherit him, since he is his beloved disciple, over being inherited by a son who is unworthy; and we find that men of deed among the children of Israel did things of this nature." "<b>And the second</b> — along these lines: that besides his being pained over the seed, he was also pained over the statements of the son of his house, who was saying that he is the one who would inherit; and this is the construal of the verse: ״וְהִנֵּה״ — for the time being he says ״יוֹרֵשׁ אוֹתִי״, and how could his heart fare well on a good day when he hears such things from the son of his house? And I found in the words of our Sages of blessed memory (Genesis Rabbah 44) that they said something akin to what I explained — that Abraham sought to go childless rather than have children who are unworthy."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:41:34.741455865Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}